/P 261 0 R
/E (5. ENGAGEMENT WITH THE INDEPENDENT BROADCASTING AUTHORITY)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (ZAMCOPS held discussions with the Independent Broadcasting Authority concerning copyright compliance and music used by broadcasters.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (The discussions addressed royalty obligations, properly licensed music and cooperation between broadcasters and the Society. Radio and television remain major users of music and important channels for promoting local artists. Promotion, however, cannot replace payment for copyright use.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (Accurate music usage records are necessary for identifying works and distributing royalties fairly. Continued cooperation with broadcasting institutions and the Authority is therefore important.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (3. COPYRIGHT LEGISLATION AND POLICY DEVELOPMENTS)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (One of the most important developments during the period under review was the publication of the Copyright and Related Rights Bill, 2025, for stakeholder consultation. The proposed legislation addresses copyright protection, enforcement, collective management and related matters.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (The Bill attracted interest across the creative industry because of its implications for the ownership, management and commercial use of creative works. ZAMCOPS publicly called for more time for creators and other stakeholders to study the proposals and submit their views.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (The Board recognises the need for copyright legislation that responds to changes in technology and protects rights holders. Reform must also reflect the practical experience of creators and the institutions responsible for administering their rights. The Society should continue to examine proposed changes carefully and participate in appropriate consultations.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (4. MUSIC LICENSING AND COPYRIGHT COMPLIANCE)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (Copyright licensing remains the principal source of revenue for the Society. ZAMCOPS licenses establishments that use copyrighted music commercially, including broadcasters, hotels, restaurants, bars, shops and entertainment venues.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (Compliance among music users remains a serious concern. Many radio and television stations have been slow to pay for the music they broadcast, and the weakened economy and load shedding have pushed compliance among other licensees to its lowest. To address this, the Society has engaged the Independent Broadcasting Authority and, following the legal action taken against ZNBC, has extended the same legal route to other media houses that owe it substantial sums.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (When commercial users fail to pay, the funds available for distribution to rights holders are affected. Improving compliance requires continued engagement, appropriate enforcement and clear public education. Buying music for personal use does not automatically authorise its commercial public use.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (6. COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT AND ORIGINAL WORKS)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (The unauthorised use of protected musical works remains a concern. ZAMCOPS publicly cautioned artists about using existing musical compositions, including protected melodies and other elements, without proper permission.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (Technology has made music production and distribution easier, but it has also increased the need to understand ownership and clearance requirements. The availability of a work online does not mean it can be reproduced or exploited commercially without authorisation.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 261 0 R
/E (The Society should continue educating creators on originality, registration, permissions and the importance of accurate records.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
